.TH CYAML_CLONE 3 "2026-09-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_clone \- produce a deep independent copy of a DOM subtree
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

cyaml cyaml_clone(cyaml node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Copies node and all of its descendants.  The clone uses the same
allocator as the source tree, keeps the tag of every node that has
one (see cyaml_node_tag(3)), and every dictionary of the clone keeps
the member order of its source (see cyaml_dictionary_first(3)).
Changing the clone doesn't affect the original, and changing the
original doesn't affect the clone.

node doesn't have to come from cyaml_parse*().  A tree that the
caller builds directly with cyaml_create_list_mp(3) and
cyaml_dictionary_set(3) has the same nesting limit that
cyaml_parse*(3) applies at parse time, and cyaml_serialize(3),
cyaml_serialize_flow(3) and cyaml_serialize_stream(3) apply exactly
that limit as well: a tree may nest at most 500 containers (lists
and dictionaries) on any path, counting the root when it is a
container, and scalars and tags take no level.  So every tree that a
parse gives can be cloned.  A tree that nests more containers than
that is refused and the function returns NULL for it.  The source is
walked with an explicit worklist instead of by recursion, so the
call stack it needs is constant and doesn't grow with the nesting
depth.  The limit bounds the work of one call, not the stack the
caller has to provide.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
node  Root of the subtree to copy.  NULL is accepted and gives
      NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The root of the new tree, which the caller owns, or NULL when the
function runs out of memory or node nests more than 500 containers.
This function has no error string, so the caller cannot tell the two
causes apart.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_destroy (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_first (3),
.BR cyaml_node_tag (3)
